Copyright (c) 2026 Jakub Sadowski ("the Licensor"). All rights reserved.

dbdb is shareware: proprietary software that You may try, and may pass on
unchanged. Its source code is not published. Its use is free for
individuals and for smaller organisations. Larger organisations need a paid
commercial licence, and the larger AI agents that work for an organisation
are counted among its people. The terms are below.


1. Definitions

"The Software" means the dbdb programs for the desktop, the command line
and the browser as the Licensor provides them, with their documentation
and examples.

"You" means the person or the organisation using the Software. An
organisation is counted together with every organisation that controls it,
is controlled by it, or is under common control with it.

"Large Organisation" means an organisation of any kind, public bodies
included, that in its last completed financial year had

  (a) more than 10 people working for it, employees and contractors
      together, with every AI Agent that is a User counted as one of them
      as section 3 says, or
  (b) a revenue, or for a body without revenue a budget, of more than
      5 million Swiss francs or the same amount in another currency.

"AI Agent" means a system that, driven by a machine-learning model, uses
the Software by itself, in place of a person doing so by hand: it starts
the Software, gives it commands or works its interface. Every running
instance of such a system is an AI Agent of its own, and so is every
subagent that an AI Agent starts.

"User" means a person who uses the Software, or an AI Agent that section 3
counts as one.

"Commercial Licence" means a separate written agreement with the Licensor.


2. Free use

You may use the Software free of charge, for any purpose including
commercial work, if

  (a) You are an individual using it for yourself, or
  (b) You are an organisation that is not a Large Organisation.

A Large Organisation may use the Software free of charge to evaluate it,
for no more than 30 days in total.

Use on behalf of someone else counts as use by them. A consultant or
contractor who uses the Software in work for a Large Organisation needs
that organisation to hold a Commercial Licence, whatever the consultant's
own size.


3. AI agents

An AI Agent that uses the Software is a User if the model that drives it
has more than 200 billion parameters. A model whose maker has not published
its number of parameters is taken to have more.

An AI Agent is not a User, whatever the size of its model, if You run that
model on Your own hardware: on machines You own or have for Your use alone,
and not as a service that someone else runs.

So an AI Agent is left out of the count if its model has no more than
200 billion parameters, or if You run its model Yourself. Either is enough.

For section 1 (a), the AI Agents that are Users are counted as the highest
number of them that used the Software at the same time during the
financial year. Each instance, and each subagent that itself uses the
Software, is counted on its own.

Use by an AI Agent is use by whoever deploys it, or by whomever it works
for, as section 2 says of use on behalf of someone else. That person or
organisation is "You", and answers for the AI Agent keeping to this
licence. An AI Agent has no rights of its own under this licence.

An individual using the Software for themselves stays within section 2 (a)
with any number of AI Agents.


4. Use that needs a Commercial Licence

Any use by or on behalf of a Large Organisation beyond the evaluation in
section 2 needs a Commercial Licence. The README says how to get one.


5. What You may do

Within the use that sections 2 and 4 allow, You may

  (a) run the Software on any number of machines You control,
  (b) make the copies that running it and keeping backups need, and
  (c) give others complete, unchanged copies of the Software as the
      Licensor published it, free of charge and together with this
      licence. Whoever receives a copy uses it under this licence, and
      section 4 applies to them as it does to You.


6. What You may not do

Without the Licensor's written permission You may not

  (a) sell, rent or sublicense the Software, or give it to anyone else
      in any other way than section 5 (c) allows,
  (b) offer the Software to others as a hosted or managed service,
  (c) decompile, disassemble or otherwise reverse engineer the Software,
      or change it or build other software from it, except where the law
      gives You that right and it cannot be excluded,
  (d) remove or change licence or copyright notices, or
  (e) get around any technical means that tell free use from licensed use.

This licence gives You no right to the source code. Source code that
reaches You in any way stays the Licensor's and is covered by these same
limits.


7. What You make with it stays Yours

This licence makes no claim on Your data, on the projects, jobs, queries
and other files You make with the Software, or on what they produce.


8. If You like it, and if You do not

In the tradition of shareware:

  (a) If You like the Software, You "shall" praise it and pass it on:
      tell a colleague, and give them a copy as section 5 (c) allows.
  (b) If You do not, You are "obliged" to say what is wrong: send a bug
      report or a request by email, or on the project's issues page on
      GitHub. The README says where.

Both are asked in earnest and in good humour, and neither is a condition
of this licence: Your rights do not depend on them. What You send may be
used to improve the Software, without anything being owed to You for it.


9. Parts by others

The Software contains components by other authors under their own
licences, among them the Terminus TTF font under the SIL Open Font License
1.1. Those licences apply to those components in place of this one.


10. End of the licence

Your rights under this licence end if You break its terms and have not put
that right within 30 days of the Licensor telling You so. They also end, as
far as free use goes, when You become a Large Organisation; You then have
90 days to obtain a Commercial Licence or stop using the Software. When
Your rights end, You must stop using the Software and delete Your copies.


11. No warranty, limited liability

The Software is provided as it is, without warranty of any kind, express or
implied, including fitness for a particular purpose. You are responsible
for checking what it does to Your data. As far as the law allows, the
Licensor is not liable for any damage that comes from using the Software or
from not being able to use it. Liability that the law does not allow to be
excluded, such as for intent or gross negligence, is not affected.


12. Law and courts

This licence is governed by Swiss law, without its rules on conflict of
laws. The courts at the Licensor's place of residence have exclusive
